notes=['Recovered finite hidden state, discrete time and continuously distributed transition observations.','Defined the observation density matrix, input profiles and trace probabilities.','Separated the proved equivalence theorem from the unspecified model-checking task.','Removed the suggested algorithmic starting point; retained missing logic, predicates, numerical output and complexity parameters.']
save(i,dict(title='Model checking Markov chains with continuous observations',formal='Can finite-state, discrete-time hidden Markov models with continuously distributed observations be model-checked efficiently? The source asks for verification of specifications about such probabilistic models after establishing a polynomial-time equivalence test. It does not identify a specification language, its observation predicates, a qualitative versus quantitative probability question, or the intended complexity bound. Consequently this record defines the stochastic model and known equivalence result, but does not substitute a chosen temporal logic or threshold problem for the source’s unresolved request.',definitions='A hidden Markov model has a finite state set Q and an observation space Σ equipped with a σ-algebra of measurable sets and a reference measure λ. A nonnegative observation-density matrix Ψ assigns a |Q|×|Q| matrix to each observation x. The matrix P with P_ij=∫_Σ Ψ_ij(x)dλ(x) is stochastic: every row sums to one. Conditional on state i, the probability of moving to j while emitting an observation in a measurable set A is ∫_A Ψ_ij(x)dλ(x). Time advances by one discrete step at each transition; a real observation is not automatically a holding time. With an initial row probability vector π, the probability of an observation cylinder A₁×⋯×A_t is π(∫_{A₁}Ψdλ)⋯(∫_{A_t}Ψdλ)1, where 1 is the all-ones column vector. These cylinders determine the trace distribution on infinite observation sequences. The source assumes a topological observation space whose nonempty open sets are measurable with positive measure, and piecewise-continuous density functions. Its concrete example uses Σ=R and Lebesgue measure. The finite input represents densities by profiles: strings giving a function type and rational parameters, with rational linear combinations. The example profile language Γ_GEM includes Gaussian densities, exponential densities and interval-supported monomials x^k 1_[a,b)(x), with rational endpoints and nonnegative integer k. Valid HMM inputs promise nonnegative densities and stochastic normalization, even when profile expressions use signed coefficients. A profile language is linearly decomposable when, from any finite collection of profiles, an algorithm can compute a basis chosen from that collection and rational coefficients expressing the others, in polynomial time in their encoded lengths; functions equal almost everywhere are identified. Two initial distributions are equivalent when they induce equal probabilities for every measurable set of infinite observation traces. This is the proved comparison task. Model checking would instead receive a specification and test its satisfaction under a stated probabilistic semantics; those semantics and the specification encoding are absent from the question.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='A complete acceptance condition first needs an exact specification language, observation-predicate representation, probability semantics and output requirement, and a running-time bound in the encoded model, specification and any accuracy parameters. A solution must then prove the bound for all promised inputs or establish a matching impossibility under explicitly stated assumptions. This record does not yet define that proposition. Deciding equality of two whole trace distributions, handling one chosen logic, or numerically evaluating a particular observed sequence does not by itself resolve the unspecified source question.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Continuous observations with finite hidden state',text='The state transition mechanism is finite, but each transition can emit a real measurement from a distribution depending on the hidden transition. This captures observations such as elapsed time or resource use without replacing the system by an arbitrary continuous-state process.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The established theorem is equivalence',text='Theorem 12 decides equality of the induced observation laws in polynomial time for rational initial distributions and a linearly decomposable profile language. The paper establishes that its Gaussian, exponential and piecewise-polynomial language has the required property.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Why equivalence does not specify verification',text='Equality of two probability measures and evaluation of a specification are different tasks. The theorem does not provide a language for describing events or a numerical representation for the probability of a specified event.',citation='primary'),dict(title='What is still missing from the extracted question',text='The conclusion asks about efficient model checking without fixing a logic or probability query. The full version retains this broad question. Choosing, for example, observation thresholds, time bounds or a temporal logic would produce additional mathematical choices not settled by that passage.',citation='full')],why='Would clarify which verification guarantees remain computationally accessible when a finite probabilistic system emits real measurements rather than letters from a finite alphabet.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='Polynomial-time equivalence is established under the profile-language assumptions; model checking is posed separately without a specification formalism.',citation='primary')],status='uncertain',status_note='Read the conference model, profile encodings, Theorem 12 and conclusion, and checked the full version. Exact-title and continuous-observation model-checking searches through 12 September 2026 found no later result that supplies and settles a unique interpretation of this broad question.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(status='needs_specification',reviewed_on=DATE,notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue),review_note=issue,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A verification question at the boundary between finite hidden-state systems and continuously distributed observations; existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['A finite hidden Markov model can emit real observations at discrete transitions.','The source represents their densities by finite symbolic profiles and proves a polynomial-time equivalence test.','It then asks whether specifications of these models can also be checked efficiently.','The specification language, observation predicates, probability semantics and requested complexity remain unspecified.','The revised card separates those missing choices from the established equivalence result and defines the probabilistic model without prescribing an algorithm.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
